  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Science Applications International has $3.6B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Science Applications International won 938 awards so far in the fiscal year, at an average award size of $3.8M, and obligations are down 18.9% year over year. Science Applications International received the largest shares from the Department of Defense and the General Services Administration, which obligated $1.4B and $1.2B respectively. Science Applications International’s top markets by NAICS are Engineering Services with $1.3B and Computer Systems Design Services with $1.2B, followed by research and development categories and several IT and manufacturing markets. Science Applications International’s fiscal-year trend shows obligations rising from $3.3B in FY21 to a peak of $4.5B in FY24, then holding near $4.4B in FY25 before the current $3.6B in FY26. Science Applications International’s profile is therefore concentrated in defense and civilian agency work, with engineering and IT services as the dominant service lines.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Science Applications International’s FY2026 obligations by agency?",
        "body": "Science Applications International has its largest FY2026 obligations from the Department of Defense, which obligated $1.4B. Science Applications International also received $1.2B from the General Services Administration, and meaningful sums from the Department of Homeland Security ($259M) and the Department of the Treasury ($188M). Science Applications International’s remaining top buyers include the Department of State, Department of Transportation, and Department of Commerce."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets dominate Science Applications International’s work in FY2026?",
        "body": "Engineering Services is the largest market for Science Applications International with $1.3B in obligations. Computer Systems Design Services follows with $1.2B. Science Applications International also has R&D obligations of $267M and $209M in two distinct research NAICS, plus smaller but notable amounts in systems manufacturing and IT-related services."
      }
    ]
  },
